Arbitrary HTTP headers are stripped for security reasons--Shiny communicates over websockets which are not susceptible to same origin policy <https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Access_control_CORS> so in general these requests cannot be trusted not to have originated from a malicious website. However in the case where you're absolutely certain a header can be trusted, there is a configuration option in Shiny Server Pro (only) that will let you allow whitelists of headers to make them available in Shiny: https://rstudio.github.io/shiny-server/latest/#proxied-headers

